>
> Do you mean I can start with the large model, after running the initial fitqtl, drop the one that have smallest %var, then run the model without that one
> (if it's a single qtl, drop the qtl, if it's an interaction, ONLY drop this interaction). Then, after running the model, if the total %var didn't change much, I'll follow the same procedure, till the total %var seemed changed dramatically? Then I can use stepwise to verify the conclusions, set maxqtl to the largest number of qtls that I started with, right?
> If I don't use p values, what seemed to be the best dvidence to drop a qtl or qtl interaction at the fitqtl step?

Yes. Or you can pass the qtl object and model formula to stepwiseqtl(), starting the multi-qtl model exploration there.

In place of those point-wise p-values, I'd focus on the LOD scores and would compare them to the genome-wide scan permutation threshold.

karl

